    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p34-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $f(x)=\\cos(2x)+\\cos(3x)$ is periodic.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Periodic: $T=2\\pi$ is a common period.\n\nThe two cosine terms repeat after $2\\pi$.",
          "worked": "The terms $\\cos(2x)$ and $\\cos(3x)$ have periods $\\pi$ and $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ respectively. A common period is $2\\pi$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x+2\\pi)&=\\cos(2x+4\\pi)+\\cos(3x+6\\pi)\\\\f(x+2\\pi)&=\\cos(2x)+\\cos(3x)\\\\f(x+2\\pi)&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nThis holds for every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, so $f$ is periodic.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Is $f(x)=\\cos(2x)+\\cos(\\pi x)$ periodic?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No.\n\nA positive period would require $T=n\\pi=2m$ for positive integers $n,m$, contradicting the irrationality of $\\pi$.",
          "worked": "Suppose $f$ has a period $T>0$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}f(T)&=f(0)\\\\f(T)&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nEach cosine is at most $1$, so $\\cos(2T)+\\cos(\\pi T)=2$ requires both terms to equal $1$. Hence, for positive integers $n,m$,\n$$\\begin{align*}2T&=2n\\pi\\\\T&=n\\pi,\\end{align*}$$\nand\n$$\\begin{align*}\\pi T&=2m\\pi\\\\T&=2m.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $n\\pi=2m$, giving $\\pi=\\frac{2m}{n}$. This contradicts the irrationality of $\\pi$. Therefore, $f$ is not periodic.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
